New option `-ffile-prefix-map=<old>=<new>`

When a path the compiler writes into its output starts with `<old>`, it is
now written starting with `<new>` instead. This covers `__FILE__`,
`__FILE_FULL_PATH__`, the file names of failed assertions, bounds errors and
`-cov`, and debug information, its compilation directory included. The same
sources then compile to the same object in any directory:

---
dmd -c -g -ffile-prefix-map=/home/me/project=. /home/me/project/src/app.d
---

records `./src/app.d`, and `.` as the compilation directory. The files the
compiler reads and writes, and its diagnostics, are not affected. As in GCC
and LDC, which have the same option, the prefix matches textually, and the
mapping given last wins.
